our other main story this morning  another Minister has quit the  government's overt resumes brexit  withdrawal agreement Sam Jamar the  universities and Science Minister  becomes the seventh minister now to  resign over the deal he's described it  as naive and one that will leave Britain  worse off for mr. jean-marc joins me now  from our westminster studio very good  morning to you good morning so we are  just 11 days away from the meaningful  vote in parliament why have you decided  to go now well I've been thinking about  the pyre the Prime Minister's proposals  over the last two weeks and I sort of  reluctantly came to the conclusion that  we actually don't have a deal at all  what we've got is a deal in name only  we've got a wish list of aspirations  which we intend to negotiate with the EU  after we've left when we've lost our  voice our veto and our vote we will have  no leverage the EU will set all the  timelines and we will be going against  27 countries my experience of the  Galileo negotiations tells me that we  are going to get horribly crippled by  the EU if we go down this path and once  that hit me it's not easy to resign it's  not easy to resign when you are doing a  job you love in government I just felt  you know it was best to let the Prime  Minister know that I could not support  her sooner rather than later  yeah Galileo is the EU satellite project  just explained to our viewers who  perhaps aren't aware of the details of  Galileo what it is specifically that  you're so angry about well so Galileo is  a sat-nav system for the EU that the UK  contributed over a billion to you now  before the ink was dry on the transition  deal the EU decided to boot as out now  if Galileo is important to the UK  because our military depends on the  security and encryption parts of it and  a lot of that was built by UK companies  now the EU shakes stack the deck in such  a way that the UK just could not carry  on having the Prime Minister's right to  say that we are terminated  those discussions and we'll build our  own now but that is a foretaste of how  the EU will behave in these negotiations  it is like going to play a football  match where the other side is here the  team playing against you they are the  referee and they make the rules as well  and so if the EU is if you're playing  against the EU and you're not on their  side it is very hard to come out in a  position where your national interest  will be enhanced I don't think anyone  would say that this is the very best  deal the Prime Minister herself accepts  that it's not the very best deal but it  is the only deal and in any divorce  there has to be compromised we've seen  Michael Gove in the press today saying  that it is the best deal that's on offer  the culture secretary jeremy wright also  saying the same thing it ends free  movement it controls farming we get  control back of Fisheries we have the  opportunity to create our own trade  deals surely this is the best that we  can do for now why can't you accept that  the mistake you're making is assuming  that there is actually a deal these are  the things we would like to achieve I  think freedom of movement credits the  Prime Minister she's done quite a lot  but everything else these are things we  would like to achieve in a negotiating  process in which the clock will be  against us we haven't as a country after  two years of the brexit vote even  decided on the big issues where do we  stand on independent trade policy versus  frictionless trade with the EU how do we  resolve the Northern Ireland issue in  the long term what we want to do on  fisheries what we want to do in  agriculture all the big questions are  still in play so we are not at the end  of the process we are actually at the  beginning of a process in which the  negotiation is to come what is being  presented as a deal is where we hope to  land in the best possible scenario I  don't believe that is credible I'm  setting ourselves up for failure ok so  you're saying that a second referendum a  people's vote can't be ruled out so why  don't you come off the fence and say  what you want is a people's vote a bit  absolutely Frank about that is an  offence sitting I I mean III think  I'm saying is that if Parliament is  deadlocked we shouldn't rule out of hand  letting the people decide and I think  that works that is in the interest of  both levers and remainders and that  doesn't in any way D legitimize the  first referendum we all know more about  the EU today than we knew then and we in  a new referendum we also know more about  the terms of departure so I think that a  if Parliament is deadlocked this could  be a way out of it the Prime Minister  has already taken one step towards this  position she's appealing to the country  to put pressure on MPs to our vote for  her deal why didn't he let the country  decide the Prime Minister has also said  that a people's vote isn't going to  happen but let me ask you this if as is  widely expected the vote doesn't go her  way in a week or so time do you think  the Prime Minister could be the person  to say okay well now is the time to take  it back to the people absolutely I think  I think the Prime Minister could do that  and she could put her deal alongside it  a number of other options to the country  I think that could break the logjam but  from how I looked at it this is the  biggest vote possibly in my political  career and I just thought I've got to  come I've got a vote in the way that I  think is right and the way that is in  the national interest and it's for  everyone to do the same and then when we  get to the other side there are other  options that note that we need to think  about rather than focus the false choice  that is being posed which is the Prime  Minister's deal or chaos okay so what  would you like to see I mean you've  touched on it partially if a Norway  model with a customs union was an option  would you be happy with that despite the  fact that of course freedom of movement  is part and parcel well the problem with  the Norway model is you sign up to  everything and you but you have you have  no voice you have no say you have no  veto so the only thing you've achieved  by  leaving the EU on the Norway model is  that you've left your veto in the room  and I think that kind of democratic  deficit and loss of sovereignty is one  that the British public would never  really accept I happen to think that I'm  as a country given that we need for our  own ambitions to have a preeminent place  in Europe we're either all in or all out  you would David Cameron's parliamentary  private secretary for a time do you  think he made a huge error calling for  this referendum in the first place well  the referendums are unpredictable you  never know what questions people are  really going to be answering and so even  when I say there should be a second  referendum I am mindful of that but I  think he felt and I spoke to him a lot  about it that there was going to be a  time when the question about our EU  membership had to be settled once and  for all clearly that is not the case but  I think what is missing from the debate  and was missing in the referendum  campaign and is still missing from the  debate is reckoning with the public  about the real trade-offs that have to  be made and I'm not saying just say it's  a compromise let's go for this but every  option involves trade-offs and we've got  to explain it to the public and level  with people about what it is and I think  in that way we'll have a more mature  debate and I think those who say that  somehow doing that would turn the public  of politics are probably under  estimating the maturity of our political  democracy
 

